# Frontend architecture (React)

## Folder structure

```
src/
  components/   reusable UI primitives, used across features
  features/     feature modules, self-contained
    <feature>/
      Feature.tsx
      Feature.types.ts
      Feature.utils.ts
      Feature.module.scss
      subcomponents/    optional, only when Feature.tsx grows
  pages/        route-level composition
  services/     API calls, grouped by domain
    <domain>.service.ts
  hooks/        shared cross-feature hooks
  lib/          framework-adjacent helpers (date, format, etc.)
```

**Dependency direction:**

- pages → import from features, components, services
- features → import from components, services, hooks, lib
- components → don't import from features or pages
- services → standalone

If you find yourself importing from `pages/` anywhere, that thing isn't page-specific — extract it to `features/` or `components/`.

## Component composition

### No boolean prop variants

Each boolean doubles the state space and breeds unmaintainable conditionals. Use explicit variant components.

```tsx
// No
<Composer isThread isEditing={false} channelId="abc" />

// Yes
<ThreadComposer channelId="abc" />
<EditComposer messageId="xyz" />
```

### Compound components for complex pieces

Components with 3+ internal sections expose subcomponents under one namespace. Subcomponents read shared state from a Provider context, not props.

```tsx
<Composer.Provider state={state} actions={actions}>
  <Composer.Frame>
    <Composer.Input />
    <Composer.Footer>
      <Composer.Submit />
    </Composer.Footer>
  </Composer.Frame>
</Composer.Provider>
```

Consumers compose exactly the pieces they need. No render props, no `showFormatting` flags.

### Lift state to providers when siblings need it

If a component outside the visual hierarchy needs the same state, the state goes in a Provider that wraps both. Never `useEffect`-sync state up. Never read from refs across the tree.

### Children over render props

Use `renderItem`-style props only when the parent must pass data back to the child (lists, virtualized rows). Otherwise use `children`.

## Services

- All HTTP/API code lives in `src/services/`. One file per domain: `script.service.ts`, `media.service.ts`.
- Services return typed results. Components call services, never `fetch` directly.
- Service errors are typed. No throwing strings, no unchecked `Error`.
- A service file shouldn't know anything about React. It's pure data access.

## Don't

- Don't import from `pages/`. Anywhere.
- Don't reach into another feature's internals (`features/foo/Foo.utils.ts` from `features/bar/`). Cross-feature reuse goes through `components/`, `hooks/`, or `lib/`.
- Don't create a feature folder for a single 30-line component. Put it in `components/` until it earns its own folder.
